    },
    {
      "id": "ukvb2ki",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Samudragupta (335–375 CE)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "99u46fi",
      "content": "• One of the greatest rulers of the Gupta dynasty."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pnhq4qo",
      "content": "• Known as the 'Napoleon of India' because of his military conquests."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3s9prwh",
      "content": "• Information about his conquests is found in the Allahabad Pillar Inscription."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "70b3q97",
      "content": "• The inscription was written by Harisena, his court poet."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ssh6ffy",
      "content": "• He performed the Ashvamedha sacrifice."
    },
    {
      "id": "005wfr2",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya) (380–415 CE)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0hl9ob7",
      "content": "• He expanded the Gupta Empire further."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0mkqt0b",
      "content": "• He defeated the Shakas and gained control over western India."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xjo6gxm",
      "content": "• His court was famous for the 'Nine Gems (Navaratnas)'."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b4lnwf4",
      "content": "• The famous Chinese traveler Fa-Hien visited India during his reign."
    },
    {
      "id": "bugqkft",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Administration of the Gupta Empire"
    },
